The writer appears to be a young, low-skilled job seeker in Hong Kong who feels excluded from the labor market. He argues that companies prefer cash-heavy offers and that applicants without experience or formal education have no real choice. His conclusion is that the shortage of jobs is not mainly due to employers offering too few positions, but because many job seekers are unsuitable or “problematic.”

I do not fully agree with that conclusion. His frustration is understandable, but the paragraph overstates the issue. It treats lack of experience and education as the main cause of unemployment, while ignoring broader factors such as economic conditions, wage expectations, competition, and limited opportunities for young workers. His view is partly a personal reflection, but it is too narrow to explain the overall labor market. [end of text]


